00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.879 align:start position:0%
the average German coal power station  emits around six million tons of co2 per  year that's about the same amount as  produced by a city like Frankfurt  everyone agrees that emission levels  need to be reduced after all fossil  fuels will still be used in the future  to produce electricity can Co to be  stored  experts have developed a process that  basically turns power station chimneys  upside down the environmentally harmful  gas doesn't escape into the air instead  it's pumped deep below the earth's  surface this procedure is called CCS or  carbon capture and storage this is how  it works co2 is separated from the other  gases emitted by power stations  liquefied and prepared for  transportation afterwards it passes  through a number of layers of rocks and  earth in pipelines and is stored in  cavities deep below the surface  the process has been the cause of much  heated debate for years those in favor  argue that we need coal power stations  to meet global energy demands and say  CCS helps produce energy in a more  environmentally friendly way especially  in countries like China with generous  coal reserves that are building large  numbers of power stations  environmentalists however say storing  co2 underground involves risks they  argue that the reservoirs in which co2  is stored underground could leak and  nobody knows for sure how the gas will  react under the Earth's surface  opponents fear that soil and groundwater  could be contaminated  here in Germany following loud protests  by local residents pilot projects have  been discontinued for the time being
 

